field charter.out_of_scope
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: "yes to all proposals" (2026-09-15)
  value: |
    Budget and cost accounting beyond one simple field.
    Multi-user permissions. Each person runs their own install.
    Replacing ProjectMan's git tracking.
    Resource leveling and Gantt scheduling math.
field charter.overall_risk
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: Risks R1-R6 in the project record, agreed 2026-09-15
  value: |
    Moderate, and mostly about me rather than the technology. The two that
    matter: learning the tool I designed instead of the job it is modelled on, and
    a single late review gate that surfaces defects at the most expensive moment.
field charter.pm_authority
  provenance: specified
  evidence: "I am being the PM...." (2026-09-15)
  value: |
    PM: me. I own scope, priorities and acceptance.
    The delivery team builds from my handoffs. It implements; it does not decide.
field charter.purpose
  provenance: specified
  evidence: "this project will produce a product that acts as a study guide, practical application, and future orginization" (2026-09-15)
  value: |
    Build a PM product that works as a study guide, practical application and
    future organization.

    Working title: AutoPM (2026-09-15).
field charter.sponsor
  provenance: specified
  evidence: Charter approval, 2026-09-15
  value: Me. Sponsor, PM and primary user.
field charter.success_criteria
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: "yes to all proposals" (2026-09-15)
  value: |
    1. Every field in the tool has a source tag and a "why" explanation.
    2. This project runs start to finish inside the tool, charter through closeout.
    3. At least one other real project is managed in it after this one.
    4. I can explain each PM artifact and when it is used without opening the tool.
