field schedule.baseline
  provenance: specified
  evidence: "I want to TRY to get this done by October 15th" (2026-09-15)
  value: |
    Baselined 2026-09-15 from the charter milestone dates. The hedges in my own
    words are kept on purpose: these are targets, not commitments.

    One change so far: scope expanded to the full lifecycle on 2026-09-15 without
    moving any date. Logged as CR1.
field schedule.basis
  provenance: unobjected
  evidence: Drafted by the delivery team 2026-09-15
  value: |
    Single-point estimates, assuming uninterrupted days. No contingency reserve.
    M4 and M5 are three weeks apart on purpose: that slack is where success
    criterion 3, a second real project managed in the tool, actually happens.
field scope.acceptance_criteria
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: From the M2 handoff acceptance list, 2026-09-15
  value: |
    Installs per-user, adds a Start Menu shortcut, uninstalls cleanly.
    Launches to a project with record #1 present.
    Every screen is generated from its definition file.
    Changing a label or a "why" in a definition file changes the app with no code
    change.
    Focusing any field shows its source, why, if-blank and example.
    Every field and every table row carries a source tag and an evidence line.
    Save writes a readable record file; unsaved changes are shown.
field scope.exclusions
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: Charter boundaries, agreed 2026-09-15
  value: |
    No resource leveling. No critical path calculation. The schedule screen draws
    the dates you enter; it does not compute them.
    No multi-user sync or permissions.
    No git tracking. ProjectMan already does that.
field scope.product_scope
  provenance: agreed
  evidence: Elaborated from the charter by the delivery team, 2026-09-15
  value: |
    A single-window desktop application. A lifecycle rail on the left, one screen
    per PM artifact in the middle, and a help panel on the right that explains the
    focused field.

    Every screen is generated from a definition file. No field is written in code,
    so changing a label or an explanation is a text edit, not a build.
